Description: Fix GHSA-q4gf-8mx6-v5v3 (CVE-2026-23869) - bound RSC deserialization work to prevent CPU DoS
 Adds a string-literal-aware O(n) pre-scan (enforceModelStringLimits) of each raw
 Flight model chunk before JSON.parse in initializeModelChunk, capping structural
 bracket/brace nesting depth (default 1000, or response._arraySizeLimit when an
 explicit limit is threaded in via decodeReply's new options.arraySizeLimit
 parameter) and, when a limit is configured, the cumulative structural node count
 across chunks (response._arrayNodeCount), throwing before JSON.parse can be
 handed attacker-controlled, deeply nested/cyclic-shaped input. Prevents
 unbounded CPU consumption when decodeReply/createResponse parse untrusted
 Server Actions / RSC request bodies. Applied to all four vendored
 react-server-dom-webpack cjs bundles (edge/node, dev/prod-min).
 .
 next@13.5.6 inlines its own copy of this same react-server-dom-webpack
 model-chunk-resolution logic into the pre-bundled runtimes it actually
 requires at request time (dist/compiled/next-server/app-page*.runtime.*.js -
 dev/prod, default and experimental, and the turbo variants). The standalone
 dist/compiled/react-server-dom-webpack/cjs/*.js files patched above are NOT
 loaded by `next start`/`next dev` for App Router Server Actions, so a depth
 guard there alone does not protect a running app. This revision adds a
 minimal inline depth-cap (same default-1000 check, no arraySizeLimit/node-
 count plumbing since these internal call sites never pass decodeReply
 options) directly before every JSON.parse(chunk.value, chunk._response.
 _fromJSON) call site inside those six next-server runtime bundles.
Origin: ai-synthesized, GHSA-q4gf-8mx6-v5v3
